WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.207
AI coding agents say done.

2
00:00:02.207 --> 00:00:03.913
ANIMA makes them prove it.

3
00:00:04.163 --> 00:00:06.365
This is how this week's work moved.

4
00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:08.441
Many agents, working in parallel.

5
00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:11.523
Some results came back rejected, and were redone.

6
00:00:13.273 --> 00:00:14.336
Now picture this.

7
00:00:14.336 --> 00:00:16.274
You bring your own coding tool.

8
00:00:16.274 --> 00:00:18.649
Claude Code, Codex, whichever you use.

9
00:00:18.849 --> 00:00:21.517
You plug it into ANIMA, with a single line.

10
00:00:21.517 --> 00:00:23.193
That part is not built yet.

11
00:00:23.543 --> 00:00:25.802
What ANIMA adds is a hidden checker,

12
00:00:25.802 --> 00:00:27.622
a ladder of stronger helpers,

13
00:00:27.622 --> 00:00:30.383
a record where any edit shows, and a memory.

14
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.214
Here is the checker, running for real,

15
00:00:36.214 --> 00:00:37.572
on a small demo job.

16
00:00:37.572 --> 00:00:39.202
The stand-in agent says:

17
00:00:39.202 --> 00:00:39.541
done.

18
00:00:40.441 --> 00:00:41.602
The checker says:

19
00:00:41.602 --> 00:00:41.943
fail.

20
00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:45.823
The agent even tries to edit the tests.

21
00:00:45.823 --> 00:00:46.442
Rejected.

22
00:00:47.442 --> 00:00:49.111
So the job climbs a rung.

23
00:00:49.111 --> 00:00:52.583
A small local model, then Claude Sonnet, then Codex.

24
00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:53.951
This time:

25
00:00:53.951 --> 00:00:54.385
pass.

26
00:00:57.335 --> 00:00:59.731
Then we ran nine real bug-fix jobs,

27
00:00:59.731 --> 00:01:01.579
from open-source libraries.

28
00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:03.778
Seven were right the first time.

29
00:01:04.678 --> 00:01:06.278
Two said done, and were wrong.

30
00:01:06.278 --> 00:01:09.158
The checker caught both, and Claude Sonnet fixed them.

31
00:01:12.308 --> 00:01:14.434
Every decision goes into a record.

32
00:01:14.434 --> 00:01:16.559
Each line seals the one before it.

33
00:01:16.559 --> 00:01:19.498
Change a single character, and the break shows.

34
00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:24.283
Next, SST, a state space tree.

35
00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:27.558
A single screen for what every agent is doing,

36
00:01:27.558 --> 00:01:28.484
and changing.

37
00:01:28.984 --> 00:01:31.940
With voice control, and loadable in Telegram.

38
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.567
All of it is design only, not built yet.

39
00:01:36.817 --> 00:01:38.396
And TRACE is the memory.

40
00:01:38.396 --> 00:01:40.172
Search that shows its work.

41
00:01:40.172 --> 00:01:42.803
A prototype link, and a small test only.

42
00:01:45.353 --> 00:01:46.689
Here is where it stands.

43
00:01:46.989 --> 00:01:47.877
Working today:

44
00:01:47.877 --> 00:01:50.350
the checker, the ladder and the record,

45
00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:51.935
for a single kind of job.

46
00:01:51.935 --> 00:01:54.091
Fixing a bug against hidden tests.

47
00:01:54.691 --> 00:01:55.408
Prototype:

48
00:01:55.408 --> 00:01:56.556
the memory link.

49
00:01:57.056 --> 00:01:57.375
Next:

50
00:01:57.375 --> 00:02:00.701
a single-line install, plug in your own tool, voice,

51
00:02:00.701 --> 00:02:01.532
and Telegram.

52
00:02:02.132 --> 00:02:04.282
To try it, the address is on screen.

53
00:02:04.282 --> 00:02:05.716
You need Python and git.

